### 2. Null-Medium Design for `X(rldimi)`

In [`src/cpus/cpu_ppc_instr.c`](file:///C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/cpu_ppc_instr.c#L2534-L2557), the body of `X(rldimi)` was replaced with an identity body:
```c
X(rldimi)
{
	/*  NULL MEDIUM: decode and retire, insert nothing.  */
	uint32_t iw = ic->arg[0];
	int ra = (iw >> 16) & 31;
	int rc = iw & 1;

	cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[ra] = cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[ra];
	if (rc)
		update_cr0(cpu, cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[ra]);
}
```

#### Rationale & Axes:
1. **Zero Insertion / Pure Identity**: `cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[ra] = cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[ra];` leaves the destination register unchanged. No bits from `rS` are shifted, rotated, masked, or inserted.
2. **Preservation of `rc` / `update_cr0`**: `rc = iw & 1` and `if (rc) update_cr0(cpu, cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[ra]);` are maintained. This isolates the test strictly to the data-transformation and bitfield-insertion axis without perturbing condition register CR0 record-form handling.
3. **Clean Compilation**: Parameters `cpu`, `ic` and local variables `iw`, `ra`, `rc` are all used. Variables `rs`, `sh`, `mb`, `tmp`, `s`, `m`, `n` are eliminated, producing zero warnings under `-Wall -Wextra`.

